this episode of legal eagle was made  possible by skillshare  learn to think like a lawyer for free  for two months by clicking the link in  the description  article 2 section 2 of the constitution  states that the president  shall be commander-in-chief and he shall  have power to grant reprieves and  pardons for offenses against the united  states  except in cases of impeachment so under  that language  can the president pardon himself that's  what we're going to find out today  legal eagles welcome back to lostplaner  where we explain the most important  legal concepts of the day  don't forget to subscribe and hit that  bell for more legal explanations  and don't forget to leave a comment in  the form of an objection today we're  going to figure out whether the  president  has the power to pardon himself but  first  let's talk about what a pardon is a  pardon is an  action bestowed on the president by the  constitution of the united states  that completely sets aside or commutes  the punishment for  a crime a pardon is an admission of  guilt  so accept the pardon the recipient must  essentially admit that he committed the  underlying crime  now a pardon only applies to quote  offenses against the united states  in other words federal crimes the  president has no authority to pardon  state crimes  one of the reasons that this is  particularly important now  is that president trump in a news  conference recently stated i have an  absolute right to pardon myself no i'm  not above the law  i never want anybody to be above the law  but the pardons are a very positive  thing for a president  i think you see the way i'm using them  and yes i do have an absolute right to  pardon myself  but i'll never have to do it because i  didn't do anything wrong  he also tweeted as has been stated by  numerous legal scholars  i have the absolute right to pardon  myself but why would i do that when i've  done nothing wrong in the meantime the  never-ending witch hunt led by 13 very  angry and conflicted democrats and  others continues into the midterms so  the question is does the president  actually have the power to pardon  himself  well as with most things in  constitutional law we should start with  the text of the constitution  here the text of the constitution  provides a clear limit on the  president's pardon power  the pardon clause provides that the  president cannot pardon anyone in cases  of  impeachment now there is an open  question as to whether a sitting  president can be indicted  or whether the only remedy when a  president has committed some sort of  wrongdoing is  impeachment in fact i did a whole video  on that there will be a link to that  down in the description  if impeachment however is the only  remedy when the president has committed  a crime  then it would make sense that the  impeachment exception to the pardon  power would prevent the president  from pardoning himself and if the  president cannot pardon himself because  that necessarily  implicates an impeachment inquiry this  principle could extend to individuals  whose actions may be relevant to that  same impeachment inquiry  for example paul manafort or mike flynn  or other members of president trump's  campaign and staff  have credible allegations against them  that may implicate the president and  therefore by necessity  that may implicate impeachment which  would prevent the president from issuing  any kind of pardon as regards  himself or as it regards the other  members of his cabinet  but the impeachment exception to the  president's pardon power has never been  tested  so it's an open question whether that  actually provides a limit against the  president pardoning himself or not  but on top of that there is also a  general principle in u.s law  that a person cannot be the judge in  their own case  in fact in 1974 when president nixon  asked the  office of legal counsel the olc to  determine if he could pardon himself  the olc relied on that principle in  saying that president nixon could not  pardon himself  in the famous august 5th 1974 memo  mary lawton the acting assistant  attorney general wrote quote  under the fundamental rule that no one  may be the judge in his own case  the president cannot pardon himself four  days later  nixon resigned in disgrace so given that  president nixon's staff opined that he  could not pardon himself and that even  president nixon would not go so far as  an issue of self-pardon  that is potentially persuasive authority  that the president doesn't have the  power to self-pardon  now there's really only one supreme  court case that's on point in this  particular case  and that is the ex parte garland case  from 1866.  this is a supreme court case that often  gets used as an example of how the  president's ability to pardon is  basically unfettered  but i think there's actually a different  principle that is more important here  the ex parte garland case concerned  congress's ability to exclude members of  the confederacy  from practicing federal law after the  civil war  and whether the president could pardon a  confederate senator the supreme court  held five to four  that the president could in fact pardon  the confederate senator  but more importantly the court held  unanimously that the supreme court has  the authority to  interpret the scope of the pardon power  like the bedrock supreme court case of  marbury versus madison  ex parte garland is probably less  important for the actual outcome of the  case  than the principle that the supreme  court has the overview authority  to interpret the constitution in this  case and make a decision  one way or another in other words it's  less important as to whether the  president has the pardon power in  question  than the fact that the supreme court has  the ability to review the president's  pardons in the first place  so if i was going to try to predict what  would happen if a president ever tried  to pardon himself  the way thing stands it appears that  there's nothing that would stop the  president from pardoning himself  in the first instance but under current  supreme court case law the supreme court  has the power to review that pardon  and if i had to guess what the supreme  court would do with a self-pardon  i would think that the supreme court  would actually nullify the president's  self-pardon on the grounds that either  the president cannot be the judge in his  own case  or that the impeachment exception  applies which prevents the president's  pardon from going into effect  now regardless of whether the president  actually has the power to self-pardon  it is almost universally acknowledged  that if a president ever did try to  pardon  him or herself that it would almost  certainly lead to impeachment by the  house and senate and removal from office  but that is not necessarily required by  the constitution  but frankly i think i speak for a lot of  people when i say that i hope  we never have to actually test that  legal principle in action  just to be on the safe side members of  the current administration might want to  brush up on their job skills just in  case  like for example learning a new language  like russian  i'd recommend alina sichova's skillshare  course on basic russian  i hear there are many job opportunities  for senior campaign officials in russia  and it always helps to learn the  language of your employer skillshare is  an  online learning community that has over  20 000 classes on everything  like lifestyle design and technology the  first  500 legal eagles will get two free  months of skillshare when you click on  the link below  the free premium membership gives you  unlimited access to must-know topics so  you can improve your skills  and learn new things now premiere [ __ ]  valuchi roosky yazig  so click on the link get two free months  and start learning today  and until next time i'll see you in  court
 

